7. SECURE PASSCODE-BASED ACCESS
==================================================

Implement a genuine access architecture.

Do not confuse a frontend PIN screen with authentication.

Preferred architecture:

- Supabase Authentication for member identity.
- Email OTP or magic-link verification.
- A separate, expiring invitation passcode for joining a shared healthcare profile.
- Server-side verification of invitation codes.
- Role-based access permissions.
- PostgreSQL Row Level Security.
- Server-side authorization for sensitive operations.

Invitation requirements:
- Generate unique invitation codes.
- Associate invitations with a profile and intended role.
- Store only secure hashes of invitation codes.
- Apply expiry dates.
- Apply attempt limits and rate limiting.
- Prevent reuse after redemption.
- Allow revocation.
- Log membership changes.

Never store a universal shared family PIN.

Never trust a role value supplied directly by the browser.

Never expose privileged database credentials.

Implement appropriate RLS policies so members can only access profiles and records for which they have authorization.

If real backend credentials are unavailable, build a clearly labelled local demonstration authentication mode using fictional accounts.

Never represent demo authentication as production security.

